    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=1$, $b=3$.",
      "worked": "At $x=2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(2)&=2a+b\\\\f_L(2)&=2b-a\\\\f_R(2)&=2a+b\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f(2)=5$ and the three boundary values must agree,\n$$\\begin{align*}2a+b&=5\\\\2b-a&=5\\end{align*}$$\nTwice the first equation minus the second gives\n$$\\begin{align*}5a&=5\\\\a&=1\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into the first equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}2(1)+b&=5\\\\b&=3\\end{align*}$$\nThen $f_L(2)=f(2)=f_R(2)=5$. Both branches are linear, so $f$ is continuous at every point in its domain.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
